Ground-level ozone is one of the major air pollutants worldwide, which makes a direct contribution to photochemical smog owing to its high reactivity and strong oxidation.[1] Long-term exposure to ozone can cause serious lung damage and raise the risk of early death from respiratory system diseases.[2] According to World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines for air quality, the ozone concentration during $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ exposure is required not to exceed $100\ \upmu{\sf g}/\mathsf{m}^{3}$ . However, tropospheric ozone pollution is becoming worse with the intensification of human activities, and many developing countries and some developed industrial cities suffer from pronounced ozone contamination and smog especially during sunny weather.[1a-c, 1e, 3] Also, unhealthy levels of ozone are commonly found in confined space such as cabins, offices with printers and copy machines, hospitals, water treatment plants, etc.[4] It is vitally important to study the methods and materials for ozone elimination in consideration of environmental protection and public health.
The main approaches for ozone removal include activated carbon adsorption, chemical absorption and catalytic decomposition.[5] Due to the inevitable problems of sorbent regeneration and liquid waste disposal,[6] heterogeneous catalysis is currently considered as the most effective method.[5a, 5b, 7] With regard to the practical need of air filters for ventilation, catalysts for ozone decomposition need to be highly efficient and stable under various humidity levels. And very importantly, these catalysts should be able to processed into films to fit in the current ventilation systems. Up to now, extensive work has been done on gas phase ozone elimination by employing noble metals (e.g. Ag, Pd and Au),[5c, 7g] or transition metal (e.g. Mn, Fe, Co, Ni and Cu) oxides as the active components,[7b-f, 7h, 8] which are usually supported on porous substrates such as zeolites, $A l_{2}O_{3}$ , $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and activated carbon as the monolithic catalysts to achieve low flow resistance.[5b, 7h] Among these reported catalysts, $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ appears to be the most promising one with good decomposition activity and low cost.[7c-e, 9] However, its activity experiences a drastic decline due to the accumulation of trapped oxygen species on surfaces.[7d] More importantly, $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ shows depressed activity in the presence of water vapor owing to the competitive adsorption of water on reactive sites.[8a, 10] However ozone usually coexists with water vapor not only in confined spaces but also in practical application scenes like industrial wastewater treatment plants. Although much efforts have been focused on noble metal based catalysts to achieve good activity in high humidity, the large scale application of noble metal is limited due to its high cost and scarcity.[10b, 11] Therefore, it is highly desirable to develop non-noble metal based catalysts with outstanding activity and stable performance in humid environment.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with high surface area, rich functionality and spatially arranged active sites, have emerged as the promising candidates for the removal of air contaminants like particulate matters (PM), volatile organic compounds (VOCs), ammonia, sulfur dioxide, etc.[12] In particular, with the advantages of earth abundance and low toxicity of iron, iron MOFs can serve as ideal adsorbents and catalysts for harmful gases removal, and the results have been encouraging.[12c, 13] Based on the following considerations, we hypothesize that iron(III) based MIL-100(Fe) would be remarkable potential catalysts for ozone decomposition: 1) it exhibits ultrahigh specific surface area and suitable pore size for the rapid adsorption of ozone; 2) the highly accessible Fe active sites uniformly distributed in the MOF backbone are extremely beneficial for ozone decomposition; 3) it is water stable; and 4) MOF can serve as porous supports as well as active components.[13b, 14]
Herein, for the first time, we present the unique advantages of MIL-100(Fe) for ozone elimination under conditions of high humidity. MIL-100(Fe) shows outstanding and stable catalytic efficiency, with ozone conversion degree at $100\%$ for at least 100 continuous hours under the conditions of 45 ppm inlet ozone concentration, $1.9\times10^{5}~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ space velocity, $45\%$ RH and room temperature, which outperforms many adsorbents and catalysts like activated carbon and $\mathsf{a}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ (drop to $18\%$ and $60\%$ of ozone decomposition respectively after 12 h). More importantly, we found that water plays a synergetic role in ozone decomposition catalyzed by MOFs and total ozone removal is realized even under extremely wet condition (eg. $590\%$ RH). For further practical applications, MOF based catalytic filter is also fabricated by processing MIL-100(Fe) via a hot-pressing method (HoP method), and as a filter in a mask it shows almost complete protection against low-level ozone (Scheme 1).
In the present work, MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized from $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and trimesic acid according to the previous method (Figure S1).[15] The as-prepared nanosized MIL-100(Fe) has high crystallinity and its XRD pattern is well consistent with the simulated one (Figure 1b). Prior to the use of MIL-100(Fe) in ozone decomposition, activation was performed at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ with residual solvent molecules removed. ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption experiment proves the highly porous characteristic of MIL-100(Fe), with a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller specific surface area of $1726\ \mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ and accessible pore diameter of $1.68~\mathsf{n m}$ , which are large enough for ozone molecules to diffuse freely and be selectively absorbed on active sites (Figure 1c). The catalytic activity was evaluated at room temperature in a fixed bed continuous reactor with inlet gas flow rate of 1000 mL/min and initial ozone concentration of 45 ppm by using 8 W UV lamp as ozone generator. The relative humidity (RH) varying from $<5\%$ t $0>90\%$ was achieved by adjusting the gas flow through water bubbling bottle (Figure S2).
As shown in Figure 1a, MIL-100(Fe) reveals excellent and persistent catalytic activity for ozone decomposition under the condition of optimized catalyst dosage $_{(0.03\texttt{g}}$ MIL-100(Fe) diluted with $0.27\ {\mathfrak{g}}$ quartz sand) (Figure S3). The ozone conversion is as high as $100\%$ at $45\%$ RH and maintains at this level after continuous working for $100\ h$ . It is found that MIL-100(Fe) has good stability toward ozone, with no change in structure and morphology after exposure to 45 ppm of ozone for $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ at RH of $45\%$ and room temperature, as verified by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) profile, ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm, infrared spectra (IR), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) (Figure 1, S4, S5 and S6). In control experiments the precursors of MIL-100(Fe) including metal salts and organic ligands demonstrate negligible catalytic effect on ozone decomposition (Figure ${\mathsf{s}}7;$ , suggesting the significance of MOF structure on catalytic reaction. We further compared the catalytic ability of MIL-100(Fe) with traditional catalysts like nanosized $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (Figure S8, S9, and $\mathsf{s}_{10)}$ .[5a, 7b, 7d] For $\mathsf{\Gamma}\mathsf{a-M n O}_{2}$ nanowires, we can see that the ozone decomposition goes through a sharp decrease from $100\%$ to $60\%$ during the first $4\ h$ and only $60\%$ of ozone conversion is sustained after $12\ n$ (Figure 1d). $\mathsf{a}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{F}\mathsf{e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ exhibits much lower activity with ozone elimination keeping below $20\%$ over $12\mathsf{h}$ . Moreover, activated carbon as well-known porous adsorbent, with a specific surface area of $800~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ (Figure S11), is employed to compare its removal capability for ozone with that of porous MIL-100(Fe). As we can see, the ozone decay on activated carbon decreases rapidly from $90\%$ to $47\%$ in the first $2h$ due to the adsorption effect and the oxidation of carbon surfaces until saturation, and drops to less than $20\%$ within $12\mathsf{h}$ resulting from the weak catalytic activity of carbons.[5f, 6] Based on the much more superior activity of MIL-100(Fe) than that of porous activated carbon and nanosized $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ , we may infer that both porous nature of MOF and highly dispersed $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ active sites on its backbone are crucial for highly efficient and sustained ozone elimination.
As mentioned above, it is highly desirable to develop catalysts with high and stable decomposition activity for ozone under the condition of high humidity. Here, we investigated the ozone conversion performance on MIL-100(Fe) under different relative humidity conditions with RH ranging from $<5\%$ to $>$ $90\%$ . MIL-100(Fe) as a kind of water stable MOF has been well studied as water and dye sorbents.[16] Its water stability was further confirmed in this work by soaking in water for 10 days and characterized by XRD (Figure S12). MIL-100(Fe) with exposed metal sites has high affinity to water vapor. In nearly dry gas condition $(<5\%$ RH), only $10\%$ of ozone conversion was achieved during 12 h (Figure 2a). With increasing RH from $1<5\%$ to $30\%$ , much more water molecules were adsorbed on the active sites and accordingly the ozone decomposition at $12\mathsf{h}$ increased from $20\%$ to $90\%$ . Finally, complete ozone removal was realized on MIL-100(Fe) at $40\%$ RH and retained at even higher humidity values of $50\%$ , $60\%$ , $70\%$ $80\%$ and $>90\%$ (Figure 2a and S13). It is very interesting that water plays a synergetic role in ozone decomposition catalyzed by MIL-100(Fe), which is quite different from the competitive inhibition effect of $H_{2}O$ molecule on the activity of transition metal oxide catalysts like $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ .[8b] The synergetic promoting effect of water is further evidenced by alternating between $1<5\%$ RH and $40\%$ RH. We can see that the conversion efficiency dropped dramatically when switching from wet to dry gas flow, and it recovered to $100\%$ once RH was raised to $40\%$ (Figure 2b). Oyama et al.[7d, 7e] have shown that an adsorbed peroxide species was involved in the reaction process for $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ catalyzed ozone decomposition, and reactive peroxide intermediate was also proposed for ozone decomposition on mesoporous ferrihydrite-based iron oxide by Mathew and Suzuki et al.[7b] Given the synergetic promoting effect of water molecules on the activity of MIL-100(Fe) in this work, we infer that water is favourable to the formation and decomposition of the peroxide or higher valent oxide intermediate on the surface sites of MOF.
