To explore the reaction mechanism for the MIL-100(Fe) catalyzed ozone decomposition, we performed unrestricted density functional theory (UDFT) calculations with the hybrid functional B3LYP based on a 133-atom tri-nuclear Fe(III) model of MIL-100(Fe) (shown in Figure S14). The stationary points involved and the free energy profile of the catalytic reaction have been provided (Figure 2c, S15, S16, and S17), with detailed computational methods and results presented in the Computational Section of the Supporting Information. It is demonstrated that, in moist condition, the Fe(III)-bound water in MIL-100(Fe) is easily deprotonated due to the decrease of pKa and converted to MIL-100(Fe)-hydroxide (i.e., Fe(III)-OH-) with the sextet state as the ground state (Figure S18). When the first $\phantom{+}O_{3}$ molecule is included, a hydrogen atom is transferred from Fe(III)-OH- to $\O_{3}$ with a barrier of 25.5 kcal/mol (Fe(III)-OH- as the energy reference hereinafter), followed by the rebound between the resultant Fe-O and ●OOOH radical to form the first $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ product and a $\mathsf{F e(l l){\cdots}O O H}$ complex with a barrier of $24.5\ \mathsf{k c a l/m o l}$ . Next, the Fe(II)-●OOH complex is protonated leading to a $\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{I I I}){\mathsf{-O}}^{\bullet}$ radical and a water molecule. Finally, the Fe(III)-O●● rapidly reacts with the second ${{\mathrm O}_{3}}$ , forming two $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ molecules and a regenerated ferric site. The hydrogen-atom transfer from Fe(III)-OH- (MIL-100(Fe)-hydroxide) to the first $\O_{3}$ molecule resulting in a Fe-O species and an ●OOOH radical is the rate-limiting step with an overall barrier of 25.5 kcal/mol.
It is also a necessary step to process brittle crystals or fine powders of MOFs into application-specific and commercially viable materials used in practical applications. MOF films with high performance, low pressure drop and operational flexibility are regarded as promising candidates in the control of harmful air contaminants.[12b-e] Here, we further fabricated MOF-based filter (MOFilter) by hot pressing MIL-100(Fe) nano crystals onto non-woven cloth. Figure 3a shows the photograph of MOFilter with 5 centimetres long and 5 centimetres wide. The XRD pattern of the filter confirmed the successful attachment of a dense layer of MIL-100(Fe) on the substrates (Figure 3e), which was further evidenced by the SEM and elemental mapping (Figure 3b, 3c, and S19). As a proof-of-concept, this MOF layer is sandwiched in between two non-woven fabric layers and used as the filtration in a mask for daily protection against low-concentration ozone contamination in air (Figure 3d). We tested the ozone decomposition activity using this MOFilter ( $1.41\mathrm{\mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ of MIL-100(Fe) loading) by exposing five-ply MOFilter to 200 ppb of ozone at a flow rate of $1000\ m L/\min$ and RH of $45\%$ . As shown in Figure 3f, MOFilter can decompose low concentration of ozone completely for at least $12\mathsf{h}$ with a low pressure drop (20 pa) tested by differential pressure gauge. In contrast, little catalytic activity was observed with non-woven substrates alone (Figure 3f). The remarkable performance of MOFilter for ozone elimination provides potential opportunities for practical personnel protection and occupational health.
For the first time, the possibility and detailed mechanism of catalytic ozone decomposition using MOFs have been explored. We investigated the catalytic activity under moist environment and found that ozone removal efficiency using MIL-100(Fe) can reach $100\%$ and lasts over $100\mathrm{~h~}$ under high flow rate at $45\%$ RH. The superior activity of MIL-100(Fe) was further verified by comparing with porous adsorbent (activated carbon) and transition metal oxide catalyst $({\mathsf{a}}{-}{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{n}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2})$ , which were quickly deactivated with ozone removal dropping to $18\%$ and $60\%$ respectively within $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . We found that water is favorable for ozone elimination when MOF is served as the active catalyst, [9] and full decomposition of ozone is achieved with relative humidity [10] ranging from $40\%$ to $>90\%$ . Furthermore, MOFilter as a unique catalytic filter has been fabricated and used as filtration layer in a mask to protect against low-level ozone. These findings highlight [1 the great importance of MOFs for pollution control, and might open up new possibilities for the development of new catalyst for [12 ozone decomposition.
